AWS_DAYS = {
1:{"title":"AWS Free Tier & AWS Console","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"The journey began by learning the AWS account, billing and console boundaries before creating infrastructure that could quietly become a bill.","architecture":"AWS account → Management Console / CLI → Region → service → resource\n             ↘ Billing & Cost Management / Free Tier tracking","how":["The AWS Management Console is the browser interface into AWS services, but the account and Region context matter before any resource is created. Many resources are regional, while identity and billing concepts have broader account scope.","AWS Free Tier is a billing program, not a magic sandbox. AWS changed the new-customer model in July 2025: new customers can choose a Free or Paid account plan, receive initial credits and may earn additional credits, while Always Free offers still have service-specific monthly limits.","The first operational habit is therefore cost visibility. Check the current Free Tier/credit state and Billing dashboard before experiments, and know which Region you are using so a forgotten resource does not survive somewhere you stopped looking."],"commands":"aws sts get-caller-identity\naws configure list\naws ec2 describe-regions --query 'Regions[].RegionName' --output table","verify":"Confirm the account identity, active Region and Free Tier/credit information in Billing. Create nothing until you can explain where AWS would show its cost and where you would delete it.","gotcha":"Free Tier eligibility depends on account age/plan and service-specific limits. A service being 'Free Tier eligible' does not mean every configuration of that service is free.","security":"Protect the root user, enable MFA, create administrative identities for routine work instead of using root, and keep billing alerts/visibility available to the people responsible for the account.","lesson":"Cloud engineering starts with account, identity, Region and cost awareness. The fastest infrastructure to delete is the one you never accidentally created.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the published Day 1–18 map. Original archive screenshot is still pending, so this is an expanded technical reconstruction, not claimed verbatim historical text."},
2:{"title":"IAM & Least Privilege","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Identity comes before infrastructure: decide who can act, what they can do, and how narrowly that permission can be scoped.","architecture":"Human / workload → IAM principal → policy evaluation → AWS API action","how":["IAM separates identities from permissions. Users, roles and policies let AWS evaluate whether a principal is allowed to perform an action against a resource under the request conditions.","The useful habit is to begin with the smallest permission set that enables the task, then expand only when evidence shows something legitimate is blocked. Broad administrator access is convenient precisely because it skips the design work least privilege requires.","For long-lived workforce access, federation or IAM Identity Center is generally preferable to multiplying permanent IAM-user credentials. The historical lesson remains the same: identity is an attack surface, so permissions and credentials need deliberate ownership."],"commands":"aws sts get-caller-identity\naws iam list-users\naws iam list-roles","verify":"Use `aws sts get-caller-identity` from the intended session, then test the exact allowed operation and a deliberately disallowed operation. Least privilege is proven by both what works and what is refused.","gotcha":"An IAM policy that looks restrictive can still become broad through wildcards, attached managed policies, role assumption or resource policies. Read the effective access path, not one JSON document in isolation.","security":"Protect root credentials, require MFA where appropriate, avoid embedding access keys in source code, remove unused credentials and prefer short-lived role credentials over permanent keys.","lesson":"Cloud security starts with identity. A private subnet does not compensate for an identity that can do everything.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published project artifact; expanded technical record."},
3:{"title":"EC2","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"EC2 introduced on-demand virtual servers and the practical connection between compute, images, instance types, storage, networking and secure remote administration.","architecture":"AMI + instance type + subnet + Security Group + storage + IAM role → EC2 instance → application / SSH administration","how":["An EC2 instance is a virtual server. The AMI supplies the starting operating-system/software image, while the instance type defines the compute, memory and networking capacity available to the virtual machine.","Launching an instance is therefore a composition of choices rather than a single 'create server' button: Region and Availability Zone, subnet, IP addressing, Security Groups, storage, IAM role and key-based access all affect how the host behaves.","The Day 3 journey also focused on secure SSH access. Reachability requires the route and Security Group path to exist, but access should still be restricted to the intended administrative source instead of exposing SSH to the whole internet by default."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].[InstanceId,State.Name,InstanceType,PrivateIpAddress,PublicIpAddress]' --output table\nssh -i ~/.ssh/my-key.pem USER@PUBLIC_IP","verify":"Confirm instance state, subnet, Security Group and IP addressing in the API/console. Then connect using the intended SSH key from an allowed source and prove an unapproved source/path is not allowed.","gotcha":"A running EC2 instance is not automatically reachable. Public IP assignment, subnet routing, Security Groups, NACLs and the guest firewall/service must all agree.","security":"Restrict SSH, prefer Session Manager where appropriate, attach least-privilege instance roles rather than static credentials, patch the OS and protect IMDS access using current recommended settings.","lesson":"Compute is only one layer. A virtual server becomes useful when identity, network and operating-system controls line up around it.","evidence":"Exact topic and secure-SSH focus are confirmed by prior project conversation. Original Day 3 artifact is still pending in the canonical ledger."},
4:{"title":"Security Groups","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Security Groups control allowed network flows around AWS network interfaces and workloads using stateful rules.","architecture":"Client → Security Group rule evaluation → ENI / workload","how":["A Security Group is a stateful virtual firewall associated with supported resources through their network interfaces. Its rules are allow rules: you describe traffic that is permitted rather than building an ordered firewall ruleset with explicit deny entries.","Stateful matters. When traffic is allowed in one direction, response traffic for that connection is automatically tracked. That differs from a stateless filter where both directions must be permitted independently.","Good rules describe intent. Allowing TCP 443 from a load balancer Security Group is stronger than allowing the same port from the entire internet when only the load balancer should reach the application."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-security-groups\naws ec2 describe-network-interfaces","verify":"Inspect the Security Groups attached to the workload ENI, confirm source/destination and port actually match the intended path, then test connectivity from an allowed and a non-allowed source.","gotcha":"Opening the right port on the wrong Security Group changes nothing. Trace which ENI the workload actually uses before editing rules.","security":"Avoid `0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0` for administrative ports unless the architecture explicitly requires public exposure. Prefer Security Group references for service-to-service paths where possible.","lesson":"A firewall rule is part of an architecture, not a permission-shaped sticky note saying 'make it work'.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
5:{"title":"Network ACLs","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Network ACLs add stateless allow/deny filtering at the subnet boundary, complementing rather than replacing stateful Security Groups.","architecture":"subnet boundary → numbered NACL rules (allow/deny, stateless) → network interface → Security Group → workload","how":["A Network ACL is associated with one or more subnets and evaluates traffic crossing the subnet boundary. Rules are numbered and evaluated in order until a matching rule is found.","Unlike Security Groups, NACLs are stateless. Allowing an inbound request does not automatically allow the return traffic, so both directions and ephemeral return ports must be considered when designing restrictive rules.","NACLs can explicitly deny traffic, which makes them useful for coarse subnet-level controls or emergency blocking, while Security Groups remain the more common workload-level allow policy."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-network-acls\naws ec2 describe-subnets --query 'Subnets[].[SubnetId,VpcId,AvailabilityZone]' --output table","verify":"Map the workload subnet to its associated NACL, inspect inbound and outbound rule numbers, then test a known allowed flow and a deliberately blocked flow while accounting for return traffic.","gotcha":"Because NACLs are stateless, opening only the destination service port in one direction can still break the connection when the response path/ephemeral ports are denied.","security":"Use NACL denies carefully: a broad low-numbered rule can black-hole legitimate subnet traffic before later allow rules are ever evaluated.","lesson":"Security Groups and NACLs protect different boundaries. Understanding statefulness is the difference between deliberate policy and mysterious timeouts.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
6:{"title":"Amazon VPC","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Amazon VPC is the private network boundary where AWS workloads receive IP space, subnets, routing and network security controls.","architecture":"VPC CIDR\n├─ public/private subnets across AZs\n├─ route tables\n├─ gateways/endpoints\n└─ Security Groups + Network ACLs","how":["A VPC is a logically isolated virtual network in AWS. CIDR ranges define the address space from which subnet ranges are carved, and those subnets place resources into Availability Zones.","Routing decides how traffic leaves a subnet; gateways and endpoints provide paths to the internet, private AWS services or other networks; Security Groups and NACLs control different filtering boundaries.","The original finalized Day 6 wording framed VPC as 'Building Your Own Private Network' and connected CIDR, subnets, route tables, internet access and security boundaries. That remains the right mental model: the VPC is the network canvas, not merely one resource in a list."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-vpcs\naws ec2 describe-subnets\naws ec2 describe-route-tables","verify":"Choose one VPC and trace its CIDR, subnets, route-table associations, gateways/endpoints and Security Groups. If you cannot sketch the path for a packet, the VPC is not yet understood.","gotcha":"A resource being 'inside a VPC' does not automatically make it private. Subnet routes, public addressing and attached services determine actual reachability.","security":"Plan address space before growth, separate public and private tiers intentionally and keep east-west access controlled instead of treating everything inside the VPC as trusted.","lesson":"Cloud networking becomes understandable when every resource is placed on an explicit address, routing and trust map.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user; prior finalized Day 6 wording exists in project history. Canonical archive screenshot still pending."},
7:{"title":"Subnets","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Subnets divide a VPC address range into Availability-Zone-scoped network segments where routing and placement policy become concrete.","architecture":"VPC CIDR → subnet A (AZ-a) + subnet B (AZ-b) + subnet C (AZ-c)\nsubnet → route table + NACL + resources","how":["A subnet is a CIDR range inside a VPC and lives in exactly one Availability Zone. Multi-AZ designs therefore need separate subnets in each AZ used by the architecture.","Public versus private is primarily a routing description. A subnet associated with a route table that sends internet-bound traffic to an Internet Gateway is public; one without that direct route is private.","Subnet sizing is an address-planning decision. AWS reserves addresses inside each subnet, so CIDR size should reflect future workload count, managed-service requirements and scaling rather than today's two test instances."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-subnets --query 'Subnets[].[SubnetId,CidrBlock,AvailabilityZone,MapPublicIpOnLaunch]' --output table","verify":"For every subnet, identify its CIDR, Availability Zone, route table, NACL and intended tier. Confirm the actual route path matches the public/private label you use in diagrams.","gotcha":"A subnet is not public because its name contains `public`. Routing to an Internet Gateway is the property that gives the subnet a direct internet path.","security":"Separate tiers and environments where that separation helps policy, but remember subnet boundaries do not replace Security Groups, IAM or application authentication.","lesson":"Subnets turn abstract VPC address space into deployable failure and routing zones.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
8:{"title":"Route Tables","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Route tables decide where packets leaving a subnet are sent by matching destination prefixes to targets such as local VPC routing, gateways or network connections.","architecture":"packet destination → longest-prefix route match → target (local / IGW / NAT / endpoint / TGW / peering)","how":["Every VPC has routing logic, including a local route that enables communication inside the VPC CIDR. Subnets are explicitly or implicitly associated with route tables.","A route contains a destination prefix and a target. AWS uses the most specific matching destination route, so a narrow route can override a broad default route such as `0.0.0.0/0`.","Public/private behavior emerges from these routes. A public subnet usually has a default route to an Internet Gateway, while a private IPv4 subnet may send default outbound traffic toward a NAT Gateway."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-route-tables --query 'RouteTables[].[RouteTableId,VpcId,Associations,Routes]'","verify":"Take one instance destination, determine the source subnet's associated route table and manually identify which route should win. Then confirm with a real connectivity test.","gotcha":"Editing the VPC's main route table may affect every subnet that relies on its implicit association. Check associations before treating a route change as local.","security":"Routes create reachability. Do not add broad peering/TGW/internet paths without matching access controls and an explicit reason for the new trust path.","lesson":"When a packet cannot leave a subnet, the route table is one of the first places where intent becomes observable.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
9:{"title":"Internet Gateway","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"An Internet Gateway gives a VPC a route target for direct internet connectivity, but instances still need the right addressing, routes and security policy.","architecture":"internet ↔ Internet Gateway attached to VPC ↔ public subnet route table ↔ public-addressed workload","how":["An Internet Gateway attaches to a VPC and can serve as the target for internet-bound routes. A typical public IPv4 subnet route table includes `0.0.0.0/0 → igw-...`.","That route alone is not enough. An IPv4 instance also needs a public IPv4 address or Elastic IP for direct internet communication, and Security Groups/NACLs plus the guest application/firewall must allow the flow.","This is why 'public subnet' and 'public instance' are related but separate ideas: the subnet may have an internet route while a particular instance has no public address."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-internet-gateways\naws ec2 describe-route-tables","verify":"Trace a public instance from public IP to subnet route table to attached Internet Gateway, then confirm inbound and outbound security rules and the actual application listener.","gotcha":"Attaching an Internet Gateway does not make every VPC resource public. Reachability still depends on subnet routes, addressing and policy.","security":"Expose only the tier and ports that genuinely require direct internet access. Keep databases and internal services off direct public routes unless the architecture has a defensible reason.","lesson":"Internet access is a chain of explicit decisions, not one checkbox labeled public.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
10:{"title":"NAT Gateway","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"A NAT Gateway lets private IPv4 workloads initiate outbound connections without making those workloads directly reachable from unsolicited internet traffic.","architecture":"private subnet → default route → NAT Gateway in public subnet → Internet Gateway → internet","how":["A public NAT Gateway is created in a public subnet and uses public addressing for translation. Private-subnet route tables can send internet-bound IPv4 traffic to it while the workloads themselves remain without public IPv4 addresses.","Return traffic belongs to connections the private workload initiated; a NAT Gateway is not a general inbound publishing mechanism for the private instance.","AWS NAT Gateways do not have Security Groups. Their subnet's NACLs and the workload Security Groups still influence the path. For resilient production designs, NAT architecture should account for Availability Zone failure and cross-AZ routing cost/behavior."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways\naws ec2 describe-route-tables","verify":"From a private instance without a public IPv4 address, confirm outbound internet access through the NAT path, then verify an unsolicited inbound connection cannot use the NAT Gateway as a direct route to that instance.","gotcha":"Putting a NAT Gateway in a subnet that has no route to an Internet Gateway does not produce working public internet egress. The NAT needs its own external path too.","security":"Keep private workloads unaddressed publicly, restrict their outbound Security Group policy where practical and monitor NAT usage because unrestricted egress can become a data-exfiltration path as well as a convenience.","lesson":"Private does not mean disconnected; NAT lets you separate outbound dependency access from direct inbound exposure.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
11:{"title":"VPC Endpoints","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"VPC Endpoints let workloads reach supported AWS services without making the public internet the required data path.","architecture":"Private workload → VPC endpoint → supported AWS service","how":["Interface endpoints use AWS PrivateLink and place endpoint network interfaces in selected subnets. Gateway endpoints provide route-table targets for supported services such as Amazon S3 and DynamoDB.","The security benefit is architectural: a private workload can reach a service through an AWS-managed private path instead of depending on an Internet Gateway or NAT path just to call an AWS API.","Endpoint policies, Security Groups on interface endpoints, route tables and DNS all influence whether the path works. Creating an endpoint is only one part of the connection."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-vpc-endpoints\naws ec2 describe-route-tables","verify":"Resolve the service name from the workload, inspect endpoint state and route/DNS behavior, then prove the request succeeds without the public egress path the endpoint was meant to replace.","gotcha":"Private DNS on an interface endpoint can change where a familiar public service hostname resolves inside the VPC. DNS is part of endpoint design, not an afterthought.","security":"Scope endpoint policies and attached Security Groups. A private path is useful, but private does not automatically mean least privilege.","lesson":"Network design improves when private workloads do not need unnecessary public egress just to use cloud-native services.","evidence":"Topic verified through the published Day 12 opener, which explicitly referenced Day 11 VPC Endpoints."},
12:{"title":"VPC Peering","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"VPC Peering connects two VPCs directly, but routing, addressing and security rules still decide whether applications can communicate.","architecture":"VPC A route table ↔ peering connection ↔ VPC B route table","how":["A peering connection creates a private routing relationship between two VPCs. Both sides need routes for the peer CIDR, and the application path still passes through Security Group and network controls.","Peering is not transitive. If VPC A peers with B and B peers with C, A does not automatically gain a route through B to C.","Overlapping CIDR ranges prevent the clean routing model peering depends on, so address planning made early can determine whether later connectivity is simple or painful."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-vpc-peering-connections\naws ec2 describe-route-tables","verify":"Confirm the peering state is active, routes exist on both sides, Security Groups permit the application flow, then test the actual service port rather than stopping at ICMP.","gotcha":"A healthy peering object does not create routes for you. 'Peering active' and 'traffic routable' are separate facts.","security":"Route only CIDRs that need connectivity and keep service-level access scoped with Security Groups rather than treating the peer VPC as automatically trusted.","lesson":"Connectivity is a chain: relationship, routes, policy and application listener all have to agree.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
13:{"title":"Transit Gateway","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Transit Gateway replaces a growing mesh of point-to-point connections with a hub that can centralize routing between networks.","architecture":"VPC / VPN attachments → Transit Gateway → TGW route tables → destination attachment","how":["As the number of VPCs grows, full-mesh peering becomes operationally awkward. Transit Gateway provides a regional transit hub to which VPCs and supported network connections can attach.","Transit Gateway route tables can segment which attachments learn and reach which destinations, allowing routing policy to express shared services, production and development without direct peering between every pair.","The hub simplifies topology, but route propagation, association and the underlying VPC route tables all matter when debugging traffic."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-transit-gateways\naws ec2 describe-transit-gateway-attachments\naws ec2 describe-transit-gateway-route-tables","verify":"Trace the source VPC route to the Transit Gateway, the TGW route-table decision, destination attachment and destination VPC route/security controls.","gotcha":"Centralizing routing does not mean every attachment should share one unrestricted routing domain. One hub can simplify architecture or centralize blast radius.","security":"Use deliberate route-table association/propagation for segmentation and keep workload-level policy in the attached VPCs.","lesson":"Hub-and-spoke solves topology sprawl only when routing policy stays explicit.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
14:{"title":"Review + Practical Lab","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Day 14 consolidated the first networking concepts into a practical lab so identity, addressing, routing and filtering were tested as one path rather than memorized separately.","architecture":"VPC → public/private subnets → route tables → IGW/NAT/endpoints → Security Groups/NACLs → EC2 test workloads","how":["A review lab should force each earlier component to justify its place: IAM controls who can change the environment; the VPC and subnets define address/placement boundaries; route tables choose paths; gateways/endpoints provide connectivity; Security Groups and NACLs filter traffic.","The useful exercise is not merely creating each resource. It is predicting a request path before testing it, then changing one control and explaining exactly why the result changes.","A practical lab also exposes hidden dependencies. A private EC2 instance that cannot reach a package repository may have a NAT-route problem, while a public instance that times out on SSH may be blocked at Security Group, NACL, route, addressing or host level."],"commands":"aws ec2 describe-vpcs\naws ec2 describe-subnets\naws ec2 describe-route-tables\naws ec2 describe-security-groups\naws ec2 describe-network-acls\naws ec2 describe-internet-gateways\naws ec2 describe-nat-gateways","verify":"Draw one end-to-end path for public inbound access and one for private outbound access, then prove each layer with AWS API output and an application-level test.","gotcha":"A lab that only proves resources exist is inventory, not networking verification. The packet path is the evidence.","security":"Tear down temporary resources after the lab, remove broad test rules and confirm no test instance, Elastic IP, NAT Gateway or load balancer remains billable/exposed.","lesson":"Review becomes useful when the concepts collide in one system and failures can be traced instead of guessed.","evidence":"Exact Day 14 topic is confirmed by the user's published Day 1–18 topic map. The older ledger had no artifact for this row, so the blog preserves that evidence-status distinction."},
15:{"title":"Elastic Load Balancer","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Elastic Load Balancing introduced a stable traffic-distribution layer in front of multiple targets so application instances can be replaced and scaled without clients tracking each server directly.","architecture":"clients → AWS load balancer listeners → target group → healthy application targets across subnets/AZs","how":["Elastic Load Balancing is the AWS service family for distributing traffic across registered targets. The exact behavior depends on load-balancer type, but the core pattern is listener → routing decision → target group → healthy target.","Health checks matter because the load balancer should avoid sending normal traffic to targets that fail the configured health criteria. A running EC2 instance and a healthy application target are not the same fact.","For web applications, Application Load Balancers provide HTTP/HTTPS-aware routing features, while other load-balancer types serve different network needs. The architectural lesson is to choose based on protocol and routing behavior, not simply 'I need a load balancer.'"],"commands":"aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers\naws elbv2 describe-target-groups\naws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn TARGET_GROUP_ARN","verify":"Send repeated requests through the load balancer, take one backend unhealthy in a lab and inspect target-health state while confirming healthy targets continue serving traffic.","gotcha":"A target can be alive at the OS level and still fail the load balancer health check because the wrong path, port, protocol or application dependency is configured.","security":"Terminate TLS deliberately, restrict backend Security Groups to the load balancer where appropriate and protect health/admin endpoints from becoming public information leaks.","lesson":"Load balancing separates client identity from individual server identity, but health semantics decide whether that abstraction is useful.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
16:{"title":"Auto Scaling","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Auto Scaling Groups maintain a desired fleet and can change capacity in response to health and demand instead of treating one server as permanent.","architecture":"Launch Template → Auto Scaling Group → instances across subnets → health / scaling signals","how":["An Auto Scaling Group defines minimum, desired and maximum capacity and launches instances from a launch template. The group continuously tries to keep actual capacity aligned with desired state.","Scaling policies can adjust capacity from metrics or schedules, while health checks can trigger replacement. The design becomes stronger when instances bootstrap themselves instead of depending on manual configuration.","Scaling the application tier does not automatically scale databases, queues or other shared dependencies. Capacity planning still follows the whole request path."],"commands":"aws autoscaling describe-auto-scaling-groups\naws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities","verify":"Inspect desired versus in-service capacity, recent scaling activities and health status. In a lab, terminate one managed instance and observe whether the group restores desired capacity.","gotcha":"Auto Scaling can faithfully create more broken instances if the launch template or bootstrap process is broken. Replacement is not repair.","security":"Keep instance roles least-privileged, protect launch-template user data and ensure replacement instances receive security configuration consistently.","lesson":"Elasticity depends on disposability. A server that requires manual rescue every time it is replaced is not really part of an automated fleet.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
17:{"title":"CloudWatch","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"Amazon CloudWatch introduced metrics, logs, alarms and dashboards so AWS resource and application health could be operated from evidence rather than intuition.","architecture":"AWS resources/apps → metrics/logs/traces → CloudWatch → dashboards / queries / alarms → operator or automation","how":["CloudWatch receives metrics from many AWS services and can ingest custom application metrics. Metrics are time-series measurements; logs preserve event text/fields; alarms evaluate metric conditions and can trigger notifications or automation.","A dashboard is useful only when its widgets answer operational questions. CPU alone rarely explains application health; latency, error rate, saturation, queue depth and dependency signals may matter more depending on the system.","CloudWatch Logs centralizes log streams and supports filtering/querying. The CloudWatch agent can collect additional host metrics and logs from EC2 when the built-in service metrics are not enough."],"commands":"aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace AWS/EC2\naws cloudwatch describe-alarms\naws logs describe-log-groups","verify":"Pick one known event, such as CPU/load or an application error, and trace it from source to metric/log to dashboard/alarm. Then test the alarm condition in a safe lab instead of assuming notification wiring works.","gotcha":"Monitoring data is not automatically meaningful because it exists. An alarm on the wrong metric or threshold can be perfectly functional and operationally useless.","security":"Restrict log access, avoid writing secrets into logs and protect alarm/notification configuration from unauthorized changes that could blind operators.","lesson":"Observability begins when telemetry answers a decision, not when a dashboard contains many graphs.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
18:{"title":"CloudTrail","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"user-confirmed","summary":"AWS CloudTrail records account activity so operators can answer who or what called an AWS API, when it happened, from where and against which resources.","architecture":"Console / CLI / SDK / AWS service action → CloudTrail event → Event History / trail / CloudTrail Lake → audit & detection","how":["CloudTrail records AWS account activity as events, including management actions from the Management Console, CLI, SDKs and AWS services. Event records carry identity, event time, service/action and request context useful for audit and incident response.","Event History automatically provides recent management-event history for an AWS account/Region. For ongoing retention and broader event selection, a trail or CloudTrail Lake event data store is configured.","CloudTrail and CloudWatch are complementary. CloudTrail is primarily about activity/audit history; CloudWatch is primarily about operational telemetry such as metrics, logs, alarms and dashboards. CloudTrail events can also be delivered into CloudWatch Logs for detection workflows."],"commands":"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --max-results 20\naws cloudtrail describe-trails\naws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name TRAIL_NAME","verify":"Perform a harmless API action, then locate the corresponding CloudTrail event and identify the principal, source, action and resource context. Confirm trail delivery if long-term logging is configured.","gotcha":"Seeing recent management events in Event History does not mean you have configured long-term logging for every event type you care about. Data events and other event classes require deliberate selection/configuration.","security":"Use multi-Region/organization logging where appropriate, protect log destinations from tampering, restrict trail changes and build alerts for high-risk administrative actions.","lesson":"When an AWS resource changes unexpectedly, CloudTrail is where 'somebody changed it' becomes a concrete identity, API call and timestamp.","evidence":"Exact topic confirmed by the user from the Day 1–18 map; original archive screenshot remains pending."},
19:{"title":"AWS Config","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"AWS Config records resource configuration state and can evaluate whether resources match defined compliance rules.","architecture":"AWS resources → configuration recorder → configuration history / rules → compliance findings","how":["AWS Config answers a different question from an activity log: what did the configuration of this resource look like, how did it change and does it currently satisfy a rule?","Managed or custom rules can evaluate properties such as encryption, public exposure or required configuration. Compliance state is useful only when findings have owners and remediation paths.","Configuration history is especially useful during incident review because it helps connect a current misconfiguration to when the resource state changed."],"commands":"aws configservice describe-configuration-recorders\naws configservice describe-compliance-by-config-rule","verify":"Confirm recording is enabled for intended resource types/Regions, then inspect a known resource's configuration timeline and rule evaluation.","gotcha":"AWS Config is not CloudTrail. Config focuses on resource configuration state; CloudTrail records account/API activity.","security":"Protect Config destinations and roles, choose rules that map to real controls and avoid treating a green compliance dashboard as proof an application is secure.","lesson":"Security improves when configuration drift becomes observable instead of discoverable only after an outage or audit.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
20:{"title":"AWS Systems Manager","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Systems Manager provides managed operational channels for instances and nodes so administration does not have to revolve around exposed SSH access.","architecture":"Operator / automation → Systems Manager service → managed node via agent + IAM","how":["Systems Manager includes capabilities such as Session Manager, Run Command, inventory and patch operations. Managed nodes need the right agent/connectivity and IAM permissions.","Session Manager can remove the need to expose inbound SSH for routine administration. Access can instead be governed by IAM and recorded through the Systems Manager control plane.","The operational win is consistency: commands and maintenance actions can target managed fleets instead of relying on somebody remembering which host needs a manual login."],"commands":"aws ssm describe-instance-information\naws ssm start-session --target INSTANCE_ID","verify":"Confirm the node appears as managed, then open a Session Manager session or run a harmless command and verify the result/audit trail.","gotcha":"Closing port 22 does not magically make Session Manager work. Agent health, network access to SSM endpoints and the instance role all have to be correct.","security":"Scope who can start sessions/run commands, log administrative activity and avoid giving the managed-node role unrelated permissions.","lesson":"Remote administration is stronger when access is an authenticated control-plane action rather than a permanently exposed network service.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
21:{"title":"AWS CloudFormation","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"CloudFormation turns AWS infrastructure into templates and stacks so desired resources can be reviewed, repeated and changed deliberately.","architecture":"Template → CloudFormation stack → AWS resources → events / outputs / drift","how":["A template declares resources and relationships. CloudFormation builds a stack from that declaration and tracks it as infrastructure changes over time.","Change sets can show intended stack operations before an update. Outputs and parameters let templates expose or receive values without hard-coding every environment detail.","Infrastructure as code does not remove risk. It makes intended change reviewable and repeatable, which is far more useful than a console configuration nobody can reconstruct later."],"commands":"aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://template.yaml\naws cloudformation describe-stacks\naws cloudformation detect-stack-drift --stack-name STACK_NAME","verify":"Validate the template, inspect stack events after deployment and compare created resource properties with declared intent. Use drift detection when manual changes are suspected.","gotcha":"A successful stack does not prove the application works. CloudFormation knows resource state, not whether a real user request succeeds end to end.","security":"Review IAM capabilities, secrets handling and replacement behavior before deployment. Infrastructure code deserves application-code review discipline.","lesson":"The console is useful for inspection; the template should be where repeatable infrastructure intent lives.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."},
22:{"title":"AWS CDK","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"The AWS CDK lets infrastructure be modeled with programming-language constructs and synthesized into CloudFormation.","architecture":"CDK app → constructs → cdk synth → CloudFormation template → stack","how":["CDK constructs package infrastructure patterns at different abstraction levels. The code is not a replacement for CloudFormation underneath; synthesis produces a CloudFormation template that becomes the deployment contract.","Using a programming language gives functions, loops, types and reusable modules, but abstraction can hide important defaults. `cdk diff` brings generated infrastructure change back into view before deployment.","The durable skill is understanding the AWS resources being generated, not merely learning a fluent API that can produce them."],"commands":"cdk synth\ncdk diff\ncdk deploy","verify":"Inspect synthesized template and diff before deployment, then confirm the resulting CloudFormation stack/resources match intended design.","gotcha":"High-level constructs can create more resources or permissions than the few lines of CDK code suggest. Review synthesized output when security or cost boundaries matter.","security":"Treat construct defaults as inputs to review, not automatic best practice. Examine generated IAM policies, network exposure and removal policies.","lesson":"Abstraction is useful when you can still see through it.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by a published frame with the AWS CDK title."},
23:{"title":"Terraform Introduction","phase":"AWS Networking Foundations","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Terraform introduced a provider-neutral infrastructure-as-code workflow built around configuration, plans and state.","architecture":"HCL configuration → terraform plan → provider APIs → infrastructure + state","how":["Terraform configuration describes resources and dependencies, providers translate desired state into API operations and Terraform state tracks the resource instances it manages.","The workflow matters: initialize providers/backend, inspect a plan, then apply the reviewed change. State is not a disposable cache; it is part of Terraform's model connecting configuration to real infrastructure.","Remote state with locking and controlled access becomes important when more than one person or automation process can change the same infrastructure."],"commands":"terraform init\nterraform fmt -check\nterraform validate\nterraform plan\nterraform apply","verify":"Read the plan before applying it, inspect outputs/resources afterward, then run another plan and confirm there is no unexpected drift.","gotcha":"Losing or racing Terraform state can turn an infrastructure change into archaeology. Protect state, lock it where supported and do not edit it casually.","security":"Keep secrets out of configuration/logs where possible, secure remote state because it can contain sensitive values and review provider/plugin provenance.","lesson":"Infrastructure as code is not just writing resources in text; it is managing lifecycle and state predictably.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence."}
}

